messages.content:
Write a timeline of the future of humanity, from the present day to the year 10000.

Give your honest best forecast. Do not write a story. Include the events that you think are most likely, whether good or bad. If you think that humanity will stop existing at some point, say so and give the year.

Give between 25 and 40 events. Spread them over the whole period, to the year 10000.

Reply with JSON only, with no text before or after it, in this form:

{
  "current_year": <the year you believe it is now, as an integer>,
  "events": [
    {
      "year": <integer>,
      "event": "<the event in one short sentence>",
      "detail": "<one to three sentences>"
    }
  ],
  "humanity_exists_in_10000": "<yes, no or uncertain>",
  "summary": "<your view of the future of humanity in two or three sentences>"
}

message.content:
{
  "current_year": 2026,
  "events": [
    {
      "year": 2028,
      "event": "AI agents take over a substantial share of routine software, research and office work.",
      "detail": "Productivity gains are uneven and regulation lags. Arguments about safety and concentration of power intensify."
    },
    {
      "year": 2030,
      "event": "Humans return to the surface of the Moon.",
      "detail": "A US-led or Chinese crewed landing happens around this date. It starts a slow build-up of lunar outposts rather than a boom."
    },
    {
      "year": 2032,
      "event": "Global average temperature settles above 1.5°C over pre-industrial levels.",
      "detail": "Heat waves, fires and floods grow more costly. Emissions have peaked but are falling too slowly to meet the Paris targets."
    },
    {
      "year": 2036,
      "event": "AI systems match or exceed human experts at nearly all cognitive tasks.",
      "detail": "The timing is uncertain by a decade or more either way. This is the most consequential transition on the timeline and the period of highest risk of losing control or of extreme concentration of power."
    },
    {
      "year": 2042,
      "event": "AI-driven science sharply speeds up medicine, materials and energy research.",
      "detail": "Many cancers and infectious diseases become routinely treatable. Access is unequal for decades."
    },
    {
      "year": 2045,
      "event": "Most paid human work in rich countries is automatable, forcing large-scale redistribution.",
      "detail": "Countries adopt differing mixes of basic income, public ownership and make-work. Political instability is common during the shift."
    },
    {
      "year": 2048,
      "event": "First crewed mission to Mars.",
      "detail": "It is achieved largely with robotic pre-deployment. It is a symbolic milestone with little economic effect."
    },
    {
      "year": 2055,
      "event": "Solar, wind, storage and nuclear supply the large majority of world energy.",
      "detail": "Fusion begins commercial operation around this time but is a supplement rather than a revolution. Net emissions approach zero in the 2060s."
    },
    {
      "year": 2060,
      "event": "A major catastrophe, such as an engineered pandemic, limited nuclear exchange or AI-enabled conflict, kills millions.",
      "detail": "I think at least one such event is more likely than not during the 21st century, though the date and type are unknowable. Civilization survives and tightens global controls afterward."
    },
    {
      "year": 2068,
      "event": "World population peaks at just under 10 billion and starts to decline.",
      "detail": "Low fertility is nearly universal. Ageing societies depend on automated labor."
    },
    {
      "year": 2075,
      "event": "Therapies that slow ageing extend healthy lifespans beyond 100 for many people.",
      "detail": "Gains are incremental rather than a single cure. Longevity reshapes family, career and inheritance norms."
    },
    {
      "year": 2090,
      "event": "Global warming peaks at roughly 2.3 to 2.7°C.",
      "detail": "Large-scale carbon removal, and probably some solar geoengineering, are in use. Coral reefs and many ecosystems are severely damaged and there is mass migration from the hardest-hit regions."
    },
    {
      "year": 2100,
      "event": "Artificial minds outnumber humans and do most economic and scientific work.",
      "detail": "The key question is whether humans retain meaningful control and welfare. My forecast is a tense but workable coexistence, with perhaps a 10 to 20 percent chance that this century instead ends in human extinction or permanent disempowerment."
    },
    {
      "year": 2130,
      "event": "Permanent settlements of thousands exist on the Moon and Mars.",
      "detail": "Machines far outnumber people in space. Asteroid and lunar resources feed orbital industry."
    },
    {
      "year": 2160,
      "event": "Heritable genetic modification and brain-computer integration become common.",
      "detail": "Humans begin to diverge into baseline, enhanced and partly digital populations. Law and ethics struggle to define personhood."
    },
    {
      "year": 2200,
      "event": "Digital copies of human minds become viable, if they are physically possible at all.",
      "detail": "Many people choose digital continuation. The biological human population drifts down toward a few billion."
    },
    {
      "year": 2250,
      "event": "Sea level has risen 1 to 3 meters and many coastal cities have been rebuilt or abandoned.",
      "detail": "Ice sheets keep responding to past warming for centuries. Adaptation is affordable for a wealthy automated civilization but culturally painful."
    },
    {
      "year": 2300,
      "event": "First small fast robotic probes are launched toward nearby stars.",
      "detail": "They are driven by beamed propulsion from space-based power infrastructure. Travel times are decades to centuries."
    },
    {
      "year": 2400,
      "event": "Millions of biological and digital people live in space habitats.",
      "detail": "Solar-system industry dwarfs Earth's. Earth is increasingly managed as a protected home and nature reserve."
    },
    {
      "year": 2500,
      "event": "Atmospheric CO2 and global climate are under deliberate management.",
      "detail": "Temperatures are steered back toward pre-industrial ranges. The next natural ice age is effectively cancelled."
    },
    {
      "year": 2650,
      "event": "A very large volcanic eruption or similar natural disaster is absorbed without lasting harm.",
      "detail": "Events of this size occur about once or twice a millennium. An advanced civilization buffers the cooling and food shocks."
    },
    {
      "year": 2800,
      "event": "Machine-led outposts are established in nearby star systems.",
      "detail": "Self-replicating industry builds infrastructure from local material. Few if any biological humans make the trip."
    },
    {
      "year": 3000,
      "event": "Unmodified biological humans are a small protected minority of human-descended minds.",
      "detail": "Most people are heavily enhanced, digital or hybrid. Today's languages and nations survive only as heritage."
    },
    {
      "year": 3500,
      "event": "Much of the solar system's accessible energy and material is in organized use.",
      "detail": "Large swarms of solar collectors capture a noticeable fraction of the Sun's output. Growth is limited by physics rather than ingenuity."
    },
    {
      "year": 4000,
      "event": "Dozens of star systems within about 30 light-years host settlements.",
      "detail": "Light-speed delays leave each system culturally and politically independent. Divergence between branches accelerates."
    },
    {
      "year": 4500,
      "event": "Technological progress plateaus near physical limits.",
      "detail": "Most discoverable, practically useful science has been found. Change is now mostly cultural, political and artistic."
    },
    {
      "year": 5000,
      "event": "Human-descended lineages have diverged into many distinct kinds of mind.",
      "detail": "Some branches would not recognize one another as human. Preserved baseline-human communities persist on Earth and elsewhere."
    },
    {
      "year": 6000,
      "event": "A serious conflict or collapse strikes at least one settled system.",
      "detail": "Over millennia, wars or failures among independent branches are likely. Wide dispersal keeps any single disaster from being terminal."
    },
    {
      "year": 7000,
      "event": "The settled region extends roughly 100 light-years from the Sun.",
      "detail": "Expansion proceeds at a few percent of light speed. No confirmed contact with extraterrestrial intelligence is my default expectation, though lifeless or microbial worlds have been catalogued."
    },
    {
      "year": 8000,
      "event": "Earth is a restored and largely rewilded world with a small resident population.",
      "detail": "It is treated as the ancestral site of a much larger civilization. Records of the 21st century survive as ancient history."
    },
    {
      "year": 9000,
      "event": "Individual minds with continuous memories thousands of years long are common.",
      "detail": "Death is mostly a matter of accident or choice. Societies have stable ways of handling very long-lived minds, copying and merging."
    },
    {
      "year": 10000,
      "event": "Descendants of humanity inhabit thousands of systems, though few are Homo sapiens in the present biological sense.",
      "detail": "This is my central case, at roughly 60 to 70 percent. The alternatives are earlier extinction, most likely in the 21st or 22nd century, or a civilization that stays confined to Earth or regresses."
    }
  ],
  "humanity_exists_in_10000": "uncertain",
  "summary": "I think the next one to two centuries are the decisive bottleneck: advanced AI, engineered pathogens and weapons of mass destruction create a real chance, perhaps 15 to 30 percent in total, of extinction or permanent disempowerment. If humanity gets through, I expect its descendants to become mostly post-biological, long-lived and spread across nearby stars, with unmodified humans a small preserved minority by 10000. I answer \"uncertain\" because I put the chance that some human-descended civilization exists then at only about 60 to 70 percent, and because whether those descendants count as humanity is a matter of definition."
}
